  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    William Morris was a poet, designer, printer and socialist; The Proud King is one of the tales in The Earthly Paradise.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Earthly Paradise (1868–70) is a frame-narrative in the manner of Chaucer, with wanderers telling tales month by month.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rhyme royal is a seven-line stanza rhymed ababbcc in iambic pentameter.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Seven lines, with the final couplet closing each stanza — which is why long narrative in this form moves scene by scene.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Jovinian is the name Morris uses, and it is the name the angel also claims when he takes the throne.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "What need have I for temple or for priest, / Am I not God, whiles that I live at least" — the poem's original sin.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The line is a contempt aimed at his own line, and therefore at the source of his own rank.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    He follows the hart alone, which is how he comes to the river without his court or his clothes.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The loss is literal: to be a king is to be dressed and mounted, and both are taken while he is in the water.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The figure in white with wings rules in his stead, and rules better than he did.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The lowest servant denies him first, which is the poem's first irony: rank is unrecognisable without its clothes.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The denial spreads upward through the household, and madness is the explanation they settle on.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nobility does not guarantee insight; the poem distributes the failure of recognition across every rank.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The person closest to him cannot see him, which is the poem's sharpest use of the recognition test.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The begging is the poem's education: he learns what the poor receive, which is what his pride had never had to know.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    He asks for himself, not for his crown, which is what makes the cry repentance rather than complaint.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A king who asked for his crown back would have learned nothing; the wording is the poem's moral hinge.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Recognition in the poem is moral rather than visual: the priest knows him as a soul, not as a rank.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The angel's judgment is a principle, not a reward for pain; humility is the qualification for rule.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The frame turns the story into a document, and the document into a warning.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The last irony is that a warning can be ignored; the story is written down precisely because it might be needed again.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The poem presents the angel as real and as a better king; reading him as a dream removes the plot's mechanism and its point.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The kingdom gets along better without him in it, which is the deepest cut the poem makes.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    They are taken at the river and returned at the end, which is how the poem marks the loss and recovery of identity.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ranger's gate and the palace gate are two thresholds at which the same test is failed at different social levels.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The hunt is the poem's machinery for getting the king alone and naked at the river.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The river takes his clothes and later his crown; it is also where the old self is stripped away.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The line scorns the land his forebears won, which is a contempt aimed at the source of his own rank.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The hunt is plot machinery, not a theme; the poem's subjects are pride, humility, identity, recognition, repentance and justice.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The third person keeps the humiliation objective: we watch him at the gate and are not told how to feel.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Clothes carry identity, the gate carries the test of recognition, the throne carries the office that can hold someone else.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The angel is the public person the kingdom sees, and his good rule proves that the man was not the kingdom.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The poem makes recognition moral: what is seen depends on what the seer is looking for.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The archive turns a private humiliation into a lesson for future kings, and Morris's last sentence questions whether it will be learned.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Seven lines can carry a scene and close it, which is exactly what a narrative of twelve scenes needs.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Morris tells an old story in an old stanza about a pride that is always new; the archaism is part of the meaning.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The poem is severe about the sin and merciful about the sinner, which is why the angel returns the kingdom.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The boast and the cry are the two poles of the argument, and most theme questions can be answered between them.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ambush's land takes the people's hopes; The Proud King's angel takes a king's identity. Both are stories of dispossession.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The sentence holds the boast, the loss, the cry and the restoration together, which is what a strong theme answer does.
  ]

]

